  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH NEXT SATURDAY/...  \nNot much in the way of change can be expected for the longer term \noutlook as the broader flow pattern promotes a continuation of \nbenign conditions. Model guidance points towards a slight rise in \nheights aloft as we progress through the middle portion of the week, \nlikely initiating a gradual warming trend for the region during this \ntimeframe. A greater upward trend in atmospheric heights is shown \ntaking place by Thanksgiving as an unseasonably strong area of high \npressure sets up west of the Baja Peninsula. The good news is, the \nposition of this ridge will put the Desert Southwest under the \ninfluence of cooler northwesterly flow, which will likely prevent\ntemperatures that are completely out of the ordinary for the end \nof November. Even though above-normal readings will make a return \nas early as Wednesday, daily highs during the back half of the \nweek should remain quite pleasant with lower desert readings in \nthe middle to upper 70s, with perhaps a few spots reaching the \nlower 80s by next weekend. With that being said however, it would \nnot be surprising to see forecasted highs for Thursday and onward\ntrend higher from their current position as the NBM tends to be \non the cooler side 6-10 days out.",
